Oh, I think I understand now.

The word "happen" when _not_ followed by the
word "to" just means the same thing as "occur"
or "transpire" or "take place".

However, the phrase "happens to" implies random
chance, low probability, or coincidence.

(If we want to get technical, it's also possible to have
the first usage of "happen" followed by a prepositional
phrase using "to", but in that case "to" would have
an object.  For instance, "What happened to you?",
the word "to" is part of "to you", so "happened"
has its regular meaning.  But when you see the
phrase "happens to" or "happened to" without an
object for "to", then its' the other meaning.)

